   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m just trying to run a simple, unstratified analysis. However, my biogeography file is not being read properly. I don&#39;t get an error message when I upload the file, but it clear from the states at the terminal tips of the tree in the output that something is wrong. It seems that the all the states that I coded in the biogeography file are being read as a different state, shifted over by 1. </div><div><br></div><div>For example, the order is &quot;New World, Palearctic, Africa, Madagascar...&quot;, and the species I specify as &quot;Madagascar&quot; are coming out as &quot;Africa&quot;. The species I coded as &quot;Africa&quot; are considered &quot;Palearctic&quot;. This is a screenshot from some of the output below:<br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-ce9SYQPqA0o/WsRkIVwR02I/AAAAAAAAAlQ/2SoG4aZCQbUcpHpQp4To1TixHx59Tr_ggCLcBGAs/s1600/Screen%2BShot%2B2018-04-03%2Bat%2B11.29.55%2BPM.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-ce9SYQPqA0o/WsRkIVwR02I/AAAAAAAAAlQ/2SoG4aZCQbUcpHpQp4To1TixHx59Tr_ggCLcBGAs/s1600/Screen%252BShot%252B2018-04-03%252Bat%252B11.29.55%252BPM.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792484693081000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1J1W8M67nHceFS2cj3bUL7\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-ce9SYQPqA0o/WsRkIVwR02I/AAAAAAAAAlQ/2SoG4aZCQbUcpHpQp4To1TixHx59Tr_ggCLcBGAs/s320/Screen%2BShot%2B2018-04-03%2Bat%2B11.29.55%2BPM.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"147\"></a></p><div><br></div><div>My geography file is in phylip format and has a tab in between the number of species and the number of states. I included pertinent code, the tree file, and the biogeography file to this post. Has anyone else had this problem?</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you in advance!</div><div><br></div><div>Jenna </div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
